<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Graus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1063">1063</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Siege of Graus · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Graus was fought in 1063 at Graus, in the Pyrenean foothills of Aragon, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-i-of-aragon">Ramiro I of Aragon</a> was killed attacking the town, held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. The ruler of Zaragoza, al-Muqtadir, was supported by a Castilian force, which according to tradition included the young Rodrigo Díaz, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a>. Ramiro's death passed the kingdom to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez">Sancho Ramírez</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He died in 1063 at <b>Graus</b>, fighting the taifa of Zaragoza, whose ruler was supported by Castile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
